<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fengjing</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fengjing, or Feng, was a capital of the Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhou-dynasty">Zhou dynasty</a> on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-river">Feng River</a>, south-west of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xian">Xi'an</a>. It was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-wen-of-zhou">King Wen of Zhou</a> shortly before the conquest of the Shang. His son King Wu built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haojing">Haojing</a> on the opposite bank, and the twin cities served together as the royal centre until the fall of the Western Zhou in 771 BCE. Excavations have uncovered palaces, workshops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> burials.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fengjing.jpg" alt="Fengjing" width="320" height="368" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Camphora · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_of_Fengjing.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
